<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Dublin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1592</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Trinity College Dublin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> is Ireland's oldest university, founded in 1592 by a charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> as its only college. For centuries a Protestant institution of the Anglo-Irish establishment, it educated figures such as Jonathan Swift and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>. Its graduates elected members to the Irish and later the British parliaments, and they still elect senators in Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-dublin.jpg" alt="University of Dublin" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: derivative work by Kwekubo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blazon_University_of_Dublin_redrawn.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
